#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171794 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution"}] :question/id #uuid "fa51ca96-4ed2-585b-88e6-244b4d8dd66d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16588" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\n1. The time interval between successive arrivals of customers in a store\n2. The number of customers in a store at a particular time\n3. The weight of a child at birth" :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Customer arrivals can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next customer enters the store.\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of customers in a store is discrete, not continuous.\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171795 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172006 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb820969-b00e-58fd-9e6d-f8c786f91b33" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "4c677102-9f65-58f2-8998-8353f81d84d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16589" :question/problem "At an electronics store, smartphone purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one sale every $20$ minutes. Rounded to four decimal places, what is the probability that the next smartphone is sold within the next $5$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{λe^{- λx},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next smartphone is purchased. Since there is a purchase every $20$ minutes on average, the rate is\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1/20\n$$\n\npurchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 1/20\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{20} \\right)\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{20}e^{-x/20},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a customer will purchase a smartphone within the next $5$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X < 5) &= \\int_{0}^{5}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{5}\\frac{1}{20}e^{-x/20}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.- e^{-x/20}\\right|_{0}^{5} \\\\\n&=-e^{-1/4} + e^{0} \\\\\n&= 1 - e^{-1/4} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.2212\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171796 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172007 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "faf45d2f-484a-56ee-af3e-c14a63721c19" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities With Exponential Random Variables on a Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "4baa7bca-acfb-5f9b-a6b4-1fc4b949555d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16590" :question/problem "At a clothing store, customers purchase clothes for newborns continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one sale every $30$ minutes. What is the probability that it takes more than $60$ minutes to sell the next set of newborn clothes?" :question/worked-solution "Since the purchases occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next purchase as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{λe^{- λx},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next set of newborn clothes is sold. Since there is a purchase every $30$ minutes on average, the rate is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1/30\n$$\n\npurchases per minute.\n\nThe time intervals between successive purchases can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1/30\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{30} \\right)\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{30}e^{-x/30},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that it takes more than $60$ minutes to sell the next set of newborn clothes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 60) &= \\int_{60}^{∞}f(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{60}^{∞}\\frac{1}{30}e^{-x/30}dx \\\\\n&= \\left.- e^{-x/30}\\right|_{60}^{∞} \\\\\n&=-e^{- ∞} + e^{-2} \\\\\n&= 0 + e^{-2} \\\\\n&= e^{-2} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1353\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171797 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e40fd627-b52a-5d60-b144-2087979533fe" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Probabilities Using the Memoryless Property"}] :question/id #uuid "821b7a41-c7ee-54fc-9b12-b13330b109ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-16591" :question/problem "A malfunctioning internet server approves connection requests continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $2$ requests per minute. If a user has been waiting for $30$ seconds to connect, what is the probability that they will have to wait at least $20$ more seconds?" :question/worked-solution "Since connection request approval occurs continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next request is approved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the user's waiting time in seconds. Since the server approves $2$ requests per minute on average (or $1$ request every $30$ seconds), the rate is\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30}\n$$\n\nrequests per second, and we can write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Exp} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{30} \\right)\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s, t$, we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big \\mid \\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{30}\n$$\n\n$s=30$, and $t=20$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > 30 + 20 \\mid X > 30) &= P(X > 20) \\\\\n&= e^{- λt} \\\\\n&= e^{- (1/30) \\cdot (20)} \\\\\n&= e^{-2/3} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.513\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "747b25be-ff4e-5b1e-abcc-5edd1edd9790" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations That Can Be Modeled Using an Exponential Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b119e750-8857-58a7-ac9f-6f9085bec09e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171478} {:answer/id #uuid "babccd90-e626-5c5b-9124-413d38313382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171479} {:answer/id #uuid "dfad0bfd-6cd6-5c89-a897-9e48c8ca7803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171480} {:answer/id #uuid "41cc4d89-d4a2-5cab-863d-9b63a205db80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171481} {:answer/id #uuid "6f68801a-b8d4-5210-b210-f8421022613f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171482}] :answer-field/id #uuid "604cf9bb-e5f2-503b-b08c-2249180495a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171477}] :question/id #uuid "d96e1ffd-8a4d-5e80-a6cb-f6d62a7321bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-96661" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\n1. The time interval between successive incidents at a particular crossroads\n2. The number of incidents in a month at a particular crossroads\n3. The speed of a car that arrives at a particular crossroads"}]}
